  "result_summary": "A series of analytical questions were posed to evaluate the clinical significance of worsening symptoms, tachycardia out of proportion to fever, and absence of focal neurological or orbital signs in a 7-year-old boy with presumed sinusitis. The results collectively support local complications of sinusitis (e.g., subperiosteal abscess, frontal osteomyelitis, orbital cellulitis, or cavernous sinus thrombosis) over uncomplicated bacterial superinfection or foreign body, due to the systemic inflammatory response. However, the absence of key physical findings (periorbital swelling, proptosis, cranial nerve deficits) downgrades the likelihood of these complications, making them less likely despite supportive systemic signs. The pattern of findings favors infectious sinusitis with possible early complication over foreign body or structural causes.",
